    {"slug": "java-exceptions", "track": "java", "title": "Exception Handling", "minutes": 18, "level": "Intermediate",
     "summary": "try/catch/finally, checked vs unchecked exceptions, and throwing useful errors.",
     "objectives": ["Catch specific exceptions", "Use finally / try-with-resources mindset", "Throw IllegalArgumentException", "Know checked vs unchecked"],
     "sections": [
         ("code", "java", 'public static int parsePositive(String raw) {\n    try {\n        int n = Integer.parseInt(raw);\n        if (n <= 0) {\n            throw new IllegalArgumentException("must be positive");\n        }\n        return n;\n    } catch (NumberFormatException e) {\n        throw new IllegalArgumentException("not a number: " + raw, e);\n    }\n}\n\npublic static void main(String[] args) {\n    System.out.println(parsePositive("42"));\n}'),
         ("h2", "Checked vs unchecked"),
         ("p", "Unchecked (<code>RuntimeException</code>) need not be declared. Checked exceptions must be caught or declared with <code>throws</code>."),
         ("code", "java", 'static int divide(int a, int b) {\n    try {\n        return a / b;\n    } catch (ArithmeticException e) {\n        return 0;\n    } finally {\n        // always runs\n    }\n}'),
         ("exercise", "Write <code>parsePort(String)</code> that returns an int 1–65535 or throws <code>IllegalArgumentException</code> with a clear message."),
     ]},
